=20
> because to me su'u could then mean nu or ni, which means that ka could=20
> become nu ce'u or ni ce'u. So why not use a more precise abstractor? Also=
,=20
> what is the point anyway? ka has at least one ce'u in it, that's pretty=
=20
> clear to almost everyone. Why do you need to use su'u here?=20
>

